deps-restorer: replace a hoisted package by renaming, where directory renames are cheap (homescoop#1128)

stage_and_swap removed the old package tree before renaming the staged one
into place, so for the whole recursive delete a reader of node_modules/<pkg>
saw it empty out file by file, then not there (an agent reading its own
package during an update). Where a directory rename is a cheap atomic move
(pnpm_fs::directory_rename_is_cheap: native, and slicc-kernel >= 1.134.3,
which the host reads from /proc/version), the old tree is renamed aside, the
staged one renamed in, and the old one deleted afterwards; elsewhere
upstream's path stays (on older kernels a directory rename copies the tree
and timed out on large packages, 12.9.1-11's 0004). New packages are still
imported in place. The upstream RemoveExisting test becomes one that an
unremovable old tree no longer fails the swap.

Upstream: not filed (Lars decides).

